Ah—that changes the provenance question, though not enough to establish causation.

If you directly supplied the Pantheon repo to Claude and explicitly instructed Claude to use it, then there is an actual information path:

\text{Pantheon repo}
\rightarrow
\text{Claude interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{?}
\rightarrow
\text{Anthropic feature}

So this isn’t merely “two completely isolated systems independently produced something similar.” Exposure occurred at the Claude product level.

What we cannot infer from that is the crucial middle:

\text{your Claude conversations}
\rightarrow
\text{Anthropic researchers/product team}

or:

\text{Pantheon idea}
\rightarrow
\text{watermark design}

Claude having access to something during your conversations does not by itself mean Anthropic engineers saw it, incorporated it, trained on it, or that the watermark was developed afterward. Watermarking language-model output also predates Pantheon; keyed statistical LLM watermarking was published years ago, so the broad mechanism has substantial prior art.

But there’s a much better way to treat this using your own provenance machinery: build a chronology rather than argue from resemblance.

You’d want the earliest timestamped Pantheon commits containing the relevant multiplexing/carrier/latent-channel ideas; dates you first gave the repo to Claude; exact Claude conversations showing how explicitly the mechanism was communicated; public Anthropic papers/patents/posts/code predating and following those dates; and the actual technical characteristics of their new system once disclosed.

Then classify individual similarities:

\text{prior art}
\quad|\quad
\text{independent convergence}
\quad|\quad
\text{possible exposure}
\quad|\quad
\text{strong provenance evidence}

Right now I’d classify it as known exposure + interesting conceptual convergence, causation undetermined.

And that’s actually a perfect job for Afterglow/provenance: Git history gives you something much stronger than recollection because the sequence of commits can establish what Pantheon contained and when.